WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:25.499
Ashley Christian: Hey everyone, and welcome back to the number 2 septic Podcast. Today, I have Brandon Ennervold on the podcast. He's from Bismarck, North Dakota, and super excited to have him on, just hear about his story. And then also we're going to talk about winter income, because of course he's from North Dakota, so they deal with winter all the time. So, Brandon, welcome to the podcast, super happy to have you on.

2
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.079
brandonenervold: Yeah, thanks for having me. Appreciate it, being here.

3
00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:35.660
Ashley Christian: So why don't you just start by telling us a little bit about you and your business, where you're located, what kind of services you offer?

4
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.290
brandonenervold: So, we're in Bismarck, North Dakota.

5
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:47.659
brandonenervold: We own a small excavating company here. We primarily do septic systems. We do basements, water lines.

6
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:52.860
brandonenervold: Pretty much anything that needs to be dug, I guess, but…

7
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:02.179
brandonenervold: But we, definitely spend most of our time replacing septics, and new septics, and mounds, and beds, and chamber systems, so…

8
00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:02.640
Ashley Christian: Hmm.

9
00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.850
brandonenervold: That's, I guess, what we do most of the time in the summer.

10
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.150
Ashley Christian: And then you got into a 2009?

11
00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:12.639
brandonenervold: Yep, we started in…

12
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:32.990
brandonenervold: We started in 2009. I had a friend of mine start a pumping company here in Bismarck, and he called me one day, and he's like, geez, you know, we're finding a lot of tanks that have a lot of problems. They're missing baffles and slow drain fields and stuff like that, and

13
00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:45.760
brandonenervold: he had asked me if I wanted to tackle any of that, and I had a skid steer attachment for my dad's skid steer to dig holes, and we got after it, and…

14
00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:52.459
brandonenervold: I guess before I knew it, I had a backhoe, and then progressed up to where I am today, I guess, so…

15
00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:58.779
Ashley Christian: Do you still work with that same friend today? Do y'all do pumping, or do you sub out to him?

16
00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:03.690
brandonenervold: We do pumping now, so that guy actually sold his company.

17
00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:12.009
brandonenervold: So… and I still work with that guy, too, but we started pumping some of our own tanks, and we…

18
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:19.670
brandonenervold: We don't do it… full-time, full-time, kind of more or less as something extra to do, I guess.

19
00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:20.360
Ashley Christian: shopping.

20
00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:30.920
brandonenervold: Yep, yep. So, I still get a lot of calls from people that don't know what's going on, and then they call me, you know, and we'll go over there and assess the problem, and…

21
00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.720
brandonenervold: Go from there, and come up with a solution, and… That's great.

22
00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:48.680
brandonenervold: If repair can be done, we… we can do it. We, choose or try to get people to realize that their systems are failing, and…

23
00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:54.489
brandonenervold: Putting in a new will definitely get them through the next 30 years, more or less.

24
00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:58.530
brandonenervold: repair is just temporary, you know? Yeah.

25
00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:05.859
brandonenervold: So, you get to working on an old septic tank, that's probably not always the best idea, but… Yeah.

26
00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:11.120
Ashley Christian: You have on your website, it says, no upselling, no unnecessary replacements.

27
00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:16.659
Ashley Christian: So it sounds like there's a lot of replacements needed where you're at, but trying to.

28
00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:17.130
brandonenervold: Period.

29
00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:30.450
Ashley Christian: walk that line with the customer? Do you have a lot of people that are like, just do the band-aid, I really don't want a replacement, I know I need it? Or how do you kind of, like, work that out with a customer?

30
00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:33.909
brandonenervold: Well, I give them all the options.

31
00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:34.480
Ashley Christian: Hmm.

32
00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.109
brandonenervold: And the pros and cons to everything.

33
00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:37.460
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

34
00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:38.810
brandonenervold: So…

35
00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:57.410
brandonenervold: up here, most… all of the houses in Bismarck, not all of them, but most of them… this whole place was pretty much built in the 90s. I mean, there's 80s houses, there's, you know, obviously, but there's a lot of 1990 houses here. That's quite old for septic tanks. Yeah. So…

36
00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:03.229
brandonenervold: When we're looking at a septic tank that's 8 feet in the ground at the top.

37
00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:11.240
brandonenervold: and the baffles are gone, H2S deterioration is rotting out the tank to the point where digging up that tank is…

38
00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.340
brandonenervold: Sometimes not the best idea.

39
00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:14.320
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

40
00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:27.209
brandonenervold: Yeah. You know, we've had it to where we've dug them up, and the tank looks fine, put a baffle in it. Two weeks later, the tank collapsed. You know, we had one collapse, and the pressure pushed water to the ceiling in the house.

41
00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:28.060
Ashley Christian: Wow.

42
00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:32.970
brandonenervold: Not exactly the best thing… the best phone call a guy can have, you know?

43
00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:39.369
Ashley Christian: Right, and so then it ends up being more money for them if they paid for the band-aid, and now they have to pay for the full replacement.

44
00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:45.999
brandonenervold: That… that's another thing I tell them all, too, is, I mean, we can… we can put in a baffle, or this or that, but…

45
00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:56.530
brandonenervold: All in all, you're not going to get away from the tank replacement, you know. We also do terra lifting, I don't know if you know what that is, or if you've heard of it.

46
00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:57.420
Ashley Christian: No.

47
00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:01.809
brandonenervold: So there's a machine called the TerraLift.

48
00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:08.560
brandonenervold: This machine that I have was built in the 90s. They still make them today, but it's subsoil fracking.

49
00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:09.370
Ashley Christian: Okay.

50
00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:13.220
brandonenervold: So, it induces 300 psi plus beads.

51
00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:23.429
brandonenervold: into the ground, to frack the ground back open, introduces air, breaks down biomat, helps soil compaction. Okay.

52
00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:26.530
brandonenervold: we can do that as well.

53
00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:36.479
brandonenervold: depends on how it was installed in the ground and the clays. Will it work? Will it not work? What have they been dumping down their septics?

54
00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:37.420
Ashley Christian: You know?

55
00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.229
brandonenervold: Do they use RIDX?

56
00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:43.370
Ashley Christian: Yeah, yes. Those flushable wipes.

57
00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:50.159
brandonenervold: Wipes, yup. So, I mean, there's a lot that goes into, can it be saved, should we save it?

58
00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:50.770
Ashley Christian: Hmm.

59
00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:51.210
brandonenervold: you know.

60
00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:53.320
Ashley Christian: So… Yeah.

61
00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:59.229
brandonenervold: It's… it's definitely been less repair work in the last 5 years than it used to be.

62
00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:01.620
brandonenervold: People are more opting out to…

63
00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:05.510
brandonenervold: Replace that system, and get a new tank, and…

64
00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:05.840
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

65
00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:06.500
brandonenervold: I mean.

66
00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:14.560
brandonenervold: How long will it really last? We put a baffle in, you know, is it 10 years? Is it 5 years? Is it 3 years?

67
00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:17.209
brandonenervold: Is it waste of money? Is it not?

68
00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:20.690
Ashley Christian: Right? Yeah, and that's just a case-by-case basis for each.

69
00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:21.300
brandonenervold: It is.

70
00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:34.330
Ashley Christian: Yeah. Correct. Yeah. You said… you said something about 8 feet down. Do you… are you bearing the tanks deeper, in North Dakota due to, like, the freeze… the freeze level in the soil?

71
00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:42.510
brandonenervold: So, most of the tanks that were put in back then were all below the footer on baseboards, most of all of them.

72
00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:59.520
brandonenervold: It's changed in the last 15 years around here. A lot of people doing grinder pumps and gravity feeding outside, that kind of stuff. So, as far as putting those tanks in the ground that deep, again, it's still cheaper to put in a tank that deep than it is to redo all the plumbing in the house.

73
00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:00.490
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

74
00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:10.199
brandonenervold: So, that's kind of what we've been doing. Depends on… you know, we find a lot of tanks that are 10 feet to the top of the tank.

75
00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:10.960
Ashley Christian: Wow.

76
00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:16.439
brandonenervold: So, depending on the ground and whatnot, there's no warranty on something like that.

77
00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:24.409
brandonenervold: The tank that we can get here in North Dakota is a 7-foot berry depth tank. They do make a couple tanks that are 10-foot, but…

78
00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:28.169
brandonenervold: You know, that's a 1,000 gallon tank, so it's only…

79
00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:32.969
brandonenervold: can only work on a 3-bedroom house around here. That's a code, so…

80
00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:38.209
brandonenervold: But, yeah, no, it just depends on what the person wants to do, and…

81
00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:39.090
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

82
00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:41.389
brandonenervold: How… how it goes around here, so…

83
00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:49.580
Ashley Christian: Are you doing those replacements, or new installations year-round, or are those more in the summer months that you do those?

84
00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:59.129
brandonenervold: We do most of them in the summer months. Now there might be an emergency or two in the wintertime. We had one last year, two days after Christmas, we…

85
00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:01.550
Ashley Christian: Oh, wow. So… Merry Christmas!

86
00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:03.840
brandonenervold: It wasn't much… wasn't much fun.

87
00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.840
Ashley Christian: Yeah, I bet not.

88
00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.670
brandonenervold: So, and that one was just a tank install. We can't do drain fields in North Dakota after the ground freezes.

89
00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:12.110
Ashley Christian: Right. Yeah.

90
00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:31.670
Ashley Christian: So that was one of the reasons I reached out to you, Brandon, to want to have you on, is because I know making money in the winter and just this… the shift of how the work comes in is such a big deal for so many companies, especially in Canada and the northern, kind of, especially the northeastern states and northern states of the United States.

91
00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:44.190
Ashley Christian: So talk to us a little bit about what was that first year like when you got into the business, and you were… this was… it was your full-time gig, and it was your first winner. Was that kind of a doozy for you?

92
00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:51.419
brandonenervold: For me, it was just fine. So before I owned the excavating company, I was a horseshoer.

93
00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:52.130
Ashley Christian: Okay.

94
00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:57.890
brandonenervold: So we… I went to Arizona. I went to Arizona to go shoe horses, and went to rope, and… and

95
00:08:57.890 --> 00:08:58.460
Ashley Christian: That's great.

96
00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:07.950
brandonenervold: And after I started this excavating company, I think the same year that I started the excavating company, I bought out somebody's snow removal business.

97
00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:08.730
Ashley Christian: Okay.

98
00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:14.209
brandonenervold: And it just so happened that we had over 100 inches of snow that winter, so…

99
00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:20.630
brandonenervold: For me, it was okay to get going with it. It's… it's always…

100
00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:31.420
brandonenervold: it's always a problem, or always can be a problem. I always try to save up enough during the summertime to where, no matter if there's no work or a lot of work, I'm okay.

101
00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:33.180
Ashley Christian: That's smart.

102
00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:45.319
brandonenervold: Because we have dry winters up here, too, and it's looking like this one might be a dry winter again. We've had pretty much 3, 4 winters in a row where we haven't had much snow.

103
00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:52.229
Ashley Christian: So that means that the snow removal would not… like, you wouldn't be doing a lot of snow removal if it's a dry winter.

104
00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:52.980
brandonenervold: Correct.

105
00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:53.470
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

106
00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:59.440
brandonenervold: Yep, and my accounts are set up on a per-inch basis.

107
00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:04.680
brandonenervold: I don't have them set up on a yearly contract. Well, I mean, they're yearly contracts, but I mean, as far as…

108
00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:10.080
brandonenervold: them paying me every month a certain sum, right? So…

109
00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:10.550
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

110
00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:26.099
brandonenervold: Pretty much. I get paid if it snows, and I don't get paid if it doesn't snow. So… so other than… other than snow removal, you know, your, emergency pumpings, your freeze-ups, broken water lines, stuff like that. So…

111
00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:32.040
brandonenervold: I tried… I tried not to excavate in the winter, but sometimes there's…

112
00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:38.259
brandonenervold: You know, a guy with 300 head of cows that broke in waterline, you know.

113
00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:38.650
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

114
00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:47.149
brandonenervold: go help that guy out and dig through 3 feet of frost to fix a water line, you know? Yeah. And it's always in January and February.

115
00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:57.950
Ashley Christian: Okay, yeah, those hard months, right after, it's like, Christmas is over, all the happy, warm feelings of Christmas are over, and then it's just cold. It's just the dead of winter for those two. Right.

116
00:10:57.950 --> 00:10:58.980
brandonenervold: Yeah. Yeah.

117
00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:13.589
Ashley Christian: Yeah. So, do you do a lot of, you said the broken water lines? Do you do any kind of, like, the, I think it's hydrojetting, where you're steaming out, maybe, like, those leach fields or anything like that in the wintertime?

118
00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:28.929
brandonenervold: We do steaming. I've got a homemade steamer that I built out of a propane tank that I use, and it comes in very handy, but yes, we… from time to time, if we can.

119
00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:44.510
brandonenervold: we'll steam out a line going to the drain field, or the septic tank, or something. I try to not do so much of that, and leave that to the guys that actually do that for a living, more or less. So, it just depends on

120
00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:47.460
brandonenervold: what's going on, I guess. Yeah.

121
00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:48.220
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

122
00:11:48.830 --> 00:12:02.710
Ashley Christian: That's cool that you went to Arizona your first winter. I was just talking to a gentleman from Alaska, and I was like, what do you do? You know, you're inseptic, what do you do when it's, like, 9 months out of the year? And he's like, oh, I just go to Mexico.

123
00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:03.290
brandonenervold: Yeah.

124
00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:13.199
Ashley Christian: Yeah, so he just, you know, he lives and does the work in the summertime in Alaska, and then he just leaves the rest of the year and enjoys himself, so that's definitely.

125
00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:13.710
brandonenervold: Yeah.

126
00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:14.899
Ashley Christian: A way to do it.

127
00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:24.480
brandonenervold: If I didn't have a family here in North Dakota, I would definitely not want to be here in the wintertime. Yeah, for sure. Yeah.

128
00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:25.889
Ashley Christian: Really good.

129
00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:40.230
Ashley Christian: What would be your best advice for a business owner that does deal with, you know, the inclement weather, especially inconsistent? What would be your best advice? I was just talking to someone this week who said that they…

130
00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:50.170
Ashley Christian: they just basically try to not bleed out in the winter. Those were their words. So, any advice for those kind of people who are really struggling with the winter slowdown?

131
00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:53.929
brandonenervold: Just try to have a big cushion going into the winter.

132
00:12:54.120 --> 00:13:13.810
brandonenervold: And… and that is right. Try not to bleed out too much, because I don't… I don't think I've ever had a winter where I wasn't lower on income than going into it, ever. You know, it is exactly what it is. Don't… don't bleed out too much. You know, keep your finances under control, for sure.

133
00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:14.300
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

134
00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:18.490
brandonenervold: You know, knowing where you are, and that's…

135
00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:21.080
brandonenervold: The only way I've been able to make it.

136
00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:28.460
Ashley Christian: Yeah, by knowing your numbers. And I'm assuming you just, you don't hire going into the winter. Do you ever have to let anybody go?

137
00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:33.850
brandonenervold: Well, so I have one employee, and I've had this employee for 6 years.

138
00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:43.620
brandonenervold: And, the way I've been doing it… now, we changed this year a little bit, but the way I've been doing it is, is, I… he gets paid on salary.

139
00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:50.660
brandonenervold: for 8 months out of the year, and then we go into winter, and then every time I call him in, he gets paid on a by-day basis.

140
00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:51.430
Ashley Christian: Okay.

141
00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:52.469
brandonenervold: is how I do it.

142
00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:53.050
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

143
00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:56.870
brandonenervold: He… the guy that works for me is…

144
00:13:57.400 --> 00:14:12.879
brandonenervold: he has his own cows and his own farm and all that kind of stuff, so he's… he's busy doing that. He doesn't want to do snow removal anymore, which is fine with me, you know, and we're… we're down on accounts this year, so I'm gonna tackle all of them myself this winter and see how that goes, and…

145
00:14:13.510 --> 00:14:23.680
brandonenervold: It's been 6 years since I've done that, so… I don't know if it's going to be a blessing or not. It's kind of nice not to have to call people and just go to work, you know, so…

146
00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:31.650
Ashley Christian: For sure. Yeah. So your plan is to call people this winter and kind of touch base, is that what you're saying?

147
00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:36.849
brandonenervold: Well, I mean, so, like, We… when we do snow removal, we get up at 2 in the morning.

148
00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:37.710
Ashley Christian: Okay.

149
00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:48.469
brandonenervold: Well, I'm calling… I'm calling everybody in, right? You're making all the phone calls, waking everybody up at 2 in the morning, hopefully everybody shows up, right? I don't have to do that this winter, I can just get up…

150
00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:49.570
Ashley Christian: I see. Yeah.

151
00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:51.449
brandonenervold: Get out the door and go to work, so…

152
00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:53.380
Ashley Christian: Yeah, you only have to worry about yourself.

153
00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:54.010
brandonenervold: Right.

154
00:14:54.010 --> 00:15:03.589
Ashley Christian: scenario. Yeah, that makes sense. Do you do any kind of ongoing maintenance contracts with any of your customers?

155
00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:07.620
brandonenervold: We don't.

156
00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:20.739
brandonenervold: never have, I guess. We've always just felt that, if they need us, they'll call us. Yeah. You know, so… and we don't really get into too much of the commercial pumping side of stuff.

157
00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:27.600
brandonenervold: So, pretty much just residential, and I tell all my customers to pump their tanks out in the spring.

158
00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:45.239
brandonenervold: That way you get your bacteria built back up before winter, less freeze-ups, you know. Most of these new systems, you know, they're 2 feet in the ground up here, which is… which is crazy to think about, right? Because we'll get 4 to 6 feet of frost on some winters up here, so…

159
00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:46.760
Ashley Christian: is crazy.

160
00:15:46.760 --> 00:15:47.720
brandonenervold: That's cool.

161
00:15:47.720 --> 00:16:01.000
Ashley Christian: crazy to think about. So, do you… you mentioned, knowing your numbers, and how that's really important, specifically if you live in an area where two different times a year, it's a very different income.

162
00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:12.339
Ashley Christian: So, is that something you just naturally did since the time you started running the business? Did it kind of take you a while to work out the numbers? I know that's kind of a hard point for a lot of companies.

163
00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:26.499
brandonenervold: I've kind of always been that way, I guess. My parents were self-employed as well. Okay. So, kind of engraved into me to know what your living expenses are, and what your equipment costs are, and

164
00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:37.750
